## Entries

### rusqlite 0.43.0 + libsqlite3-sys 0.40.0 (bundled SQLite)

- **Version:** rusqlite 0.43.0 (crates.io), libsqlite3-sys 0.40.0, built with
  the `bundled` feature; the SQLite amalgamation it compiles is public
  domain, the Rust shims are MIT (build helper `cc` 1.11.3 is MIT/Apache-2.0).
- **License:** MIT (rusqlite, libsqlite3-sys).
- **Upstream:** https://github.com/rusqlite/rusqlite
- **Reason (P011):** durable local store for `cored` (`crates/core-store`).
  `bundled` compiles SQLite in — no system dependency on any OS — with WAL,
  forward-only embedded migrations, and an integrity check on every open.
  No secret columns: credentials stay in the vault.

### hmac 0.13 + sha2 0.11 + getrandom 0.4 (P012)

- **Version:** hmac 0.13.x, sha2 0.11.x, getrandom 0.4.x (crates.io).
- **License:** MIT OR Apache-2.0 (all three; getrandom dual MIT/Apache).
- **Upstream:** https://github.com/RustCrypto/hmacs (hmac),
  https://github.com/RustCrypto/hashes (sha2),
  https://github.com/rust-random/getrandom (getrandom).
- **Reason (P012):** capability tokens for `cored` (`crates/cored`):
  HMAC-SHA256 mint/verify under the device root key; OS randomness for
  first-run key creation. No new network or system surface.

### keyring 4.2 (P012)

- **Version:** keyring 4.2.x, default `v1` feature (crates.io).
- **License:** MIT OR Apache-2.0.
- **Upstream:** https://github.com/open-source-cooperative/keyring-rs
- **Reason (P012):** device root key storage in the OS keychain
  (Credential Manager / Keychain / Secret Service). Headless CI cannot
  reach a keychain: CI runs the in-memory `mock-keyring` suite and
  honestly skips the live-keychain test; owner-local runs carry the real
  evidence (see report-P012).
